<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greco-Turkish War (1897)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thirty Days' War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a> of 1897, also called the Thirty Days' War, was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, where Christians had risen against Ottoman rule. Ottoman forces defeated the Greek army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> within weeks. Greece paid an indemnity and accepted international financial control, but the great powers then forced the Ottomans to grant Crete autonomy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1897.jpg" alt="Greco-Turkish War (1897)" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fausto Zonaro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%B6meke_Harbi_Zonaro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1878 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> signed the Pact of Halepa, which was to give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> wide autonomy under the organic law of 1868. Ottoman commissioners repeatedly ignored it, and rebellions followed in 1885, 1888 and 1889. In 1896 clashes between the Greek and Muslim communities of the island flared again, and in January 1897 inter-communal violence broke out, with the Christian district of Chania set on fire. Cretan revolutionaries declared a struggle for independence and union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, demonstrations accused King George I and the government of Theodoros Deligiannis of betraying the Cretan cause, and the nationalist National Society pressed for confrontation. In February 1897 Colonel Timoleon Vassos landed two battalions at Platanias and proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>'s union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, although six Great Powers had already sent warships to Cretan waters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> fielded about 45,000 men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> under Crown Prince Constantine, with 16,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. The Ottoman army, trained by a German military mission under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> von der Goltz and largely made up of Albanian troops, had 58,000 men in Thessaly under Edhem Pasha, and was armed with Mauser repeating rifles while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> had single-shot Gras rifles. War was declared on 18 April. After fighting at Tyrnavos on 21 and 22 April the Greek staff ordered a general withdrawal, and Larissa fell on 27 April.</p><p>Farsala was attacked on 5 May and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volos">Volos</a> fell on 8 May. At Domokos the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> gathered 40,000 men, joined by Italian volunteers under Ricciotti Garibaldi and a Philhellenic Legion, but in mid-May an Ottoman assault broke through. The road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> lay open, but Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> telegraphed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, the Ottoman advance stopped on 19 May and a ceasefire took effect on 20 May. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> the Greeks were halted in fighting around Arta and Preveza.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A peace treaty was signed on 20 September. Greece ceded minor border areas and paid heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and its economy came under the oversight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-financial-commission">International Financial Commission</a>. The Cretan uprising went on until November 1898, when the Great Powers evicted Ottoman forces and an autonomous Cretan State under Ottoman suzerainty was founded, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-george-of-greece-and-denmark">Prince George of Greece and Denmark</a> as High Commissioner. It lasted until Greece annexed the island in 1913.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the defeat exposed the country's unpreparedness and led to the Goudi coup of 1909 and then to the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a>, which preceded Greek victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912 and 1913. The war was also the first to be filmed, though the footage is lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greco-Turkish War (1897)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rebellions in Ottoman Crete and elsewhere drew Greece into conflict, and the brief <b>Greco-Turkish War</b> of 1897 ended in humiliating defeat, an indemnity and international financial control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the revolt of 1897 and the <b>Greco-Turkish War</b>, Britain, France, Russia and Italy occupied the island and forced the Ottomans to accept its autonomy under nominal suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/state/cretan-state">Cretan State</a></li></ul></section>
